Chamarel sits on a green plateau some 260 metres up and six kilometres inland from La Gaulette. It is cooler than the coast and often greener and cloudier. It is home to some of the island's best known natural sights. This is an easy unhurried day and best started in the morning.

## 1. The road up and the viewpoint

From Case Noyale on the coast road a narrow road climbs in hairpins towards Chamarel. Stop at the [Chamarel viewpoint](/en/places/chamarel-viewpoint) on the way. Below you lie the lagoon and Île aux Bénitiers and the great mass of Le Morne Brabant.

## 2. The Seven Coloured Earths

Southwest of the village the [Seven Coloured Earths](/en/places/seven-coloured-earths) are small dunes of sand in seven distinct shades formed from weathered volcanic rock. The colours are at their brightest in sunshine. The site lies within a park with an entrance fee.

## 3. Chamarel Falls

In the same park a viewpoint looks across a gorge to [Chamarel Falls](/en/places/chamarel-falls) where a river drops about 95 metres over a basalt cliff. The falls are most impressive after rain.

## 4. Up to the gorges

Continue up through the forest on the Plaine Champagne road to the [Gorges Viewpoint](/en/places/gorges-viewpoint) which looks down into the ravines of Black River Gorges National Park and out to the sea. Come before the afternoon clouds roll in.

## Practical tips

- **Drive carefully.** The roads are narrow and winding; allow plenty of time.
- **Bring a layer.** The plateau is noticeably cooler than the coast and showers are common.
- **Eat in the village.** Chamarel has several restaurants with views. We will add the ones we recommend once we have tried them.
- **Combine with the coast.** End the day back at La Gaulette or Le Morne for the sunset.
